<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>The Use of LLMs</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LLMs were used to improve writing clarity and assist with code development. Specifically, LLMs assisted in improving the clarity, fluency, and grammatical correctness of the manuscript, including rephrasing sentences and ensuring consistent terminology. Additionally, LLMs helped generate auxiliary code and scripts for data processing, experimental setup, and result visualization. However, the core research ideas, technical contributions, experimental design, and scientific conclusions are entirely the intellectual contribution of the human authors. All LLM-generated content underwent thorough human review and verification to ensure technical accuracy, scientific rigor, and alignment with our research objectives.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Analysis for structured pruning Strategies</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide supplementary details and additional analysis complementing Sec. <a href="#S3" title="3 Methods ‣ PrunedLoRA: Robust Gradient-Based structured pruning for Low-rank Adaptation in Fine-tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Appendix <a href="#A2.SS1" title="B.1 Analysis for Gradient-based Pruning versus Activation-based Pruning ‣ Appendix B Analysis for structured pruning Strategies ‣ PrunedLoRA: Robust Gradient-Based structured pruning for Low-rank Adaptation in Fine-tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a> presents the formal statement of Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 1 (Unofficial Statement). ‣ 3.2 The Robustness of Gradient-based structured pruning ‣ 3 Methods ‣ PrunedLoRA: Robust Gradient-Based structured pruning for Low-rank Adaptation in Fine-tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> together with its proof, which underscores the robustness of gradient-based structural pruning methods with respect to the overall loss. Furthermore, Appendix <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 Analysis for the Masking Pruning and Weight Update in the Problem ‣ Appendix B Analysis for structured pruning Strategies ‣ PrunedLoRA: Robust Gradient-Based structured pruning for Low-rank Adaptation in Fine-tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a> analyzes the minimizer of Problem (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In 3.2 The Robustness of Gradient-based structured pruning ‣ 3 Methods ‣ PrunedLoRA: Robust Gradient-Based structured pruning for Low-rank Adaptation in Fine-tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) and describes the procedure for pruning columns of a full weight matrix, as summarized in Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ B.2 Analysis for the Masking Pruning and Weight Update in the Problem ‣ Appendix B Analysis for structured pruning Strategies ‣ PrunedLoRA: Robust Gradient-Based structured pruning for Low-rank Adaptation in Fine-tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Analysis for Gradient-based Pruning versus Activation-based Pruning</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As discussed in Sec. <a href="#S2" title="2 Related Work ‣ PrunedLoRA: Robust Gradient-Based structured pruning for Low-rank Adaptation in Fine-tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, structured pruning strategies can be broadly categorized into two classes, both of which are widely adopted in foundation model compression <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. To better understand their implications, we provide a theoretical analysis examining how these strategies affect the overall loss. Since different approaches employ distinct criteria to measure precision, we first formalize the notion of perturbation error and analyze its influence on predictive performance. Let <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the original weight matrix and <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{{\bm{W}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{{\bm{W}}}</annotation></semantics></math> its pruned counterpart. While our discussion primarily focuses on structured pruning, we note that our analysis, in principle, can be extended to non-structured settings.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">It is important to highlight a key distinction between the two classes of methods for the sake of conceptual clarity. Although activation-based approaches can also apply a Taylor expansion and obtain the first-order gradient term, this gradient arises from the reconstruction objective rather than from the overall loss. In contrast, gradient-based pruning methods explicitly leverage the gradient of the overall loss, providing a more direct connection to the model’s predictive performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<math id="Thmdefinition1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmdefinition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">-Perturbation Error</span>)<span id="Thmdefinition1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We define the perturbation error under different pruning criteria as follows:</span></p>
<ul id="A2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For </span><span id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">activation-based</span><span id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> pruning strategies, we say the pruned weight matrix </span><math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{{\bm{W}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{{\bm{W}}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> satisfies </span><math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-perturbation error if: </span><math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\widehat{{\bm{W}}}{\bm{X}}-{\bm{W}}{\bm{X}}\|\leq\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑿</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑿</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\widehat{{\bm{W}}}{\bm{X}}-{\bm{W}}{\bm{X}}\|\leq\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,
where </span><math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑿</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{X}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is the input of the parameter layer.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For </span><span id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">gradient-based</span><span id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> pruning strategies, we define </span><math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-perturbation error as:
</span><math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{L}(\widehat{{\bm{W}}})-\mathcal{L}({\bm{W}})|\leq\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{L}(\widehat{{\bm{W}}})-\mathcal{L}({\bm{W}})|\leq\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, where </span><math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> denotes the task-specific loss function.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In Def <a href="#Thmdefinition1" title="Definition 1 (𝜀-Perturbation Error). ‣ B.1 Analysis for Gradient-based Pruning versus Activation-based Pruning ‣ Appendix B Analysis for structured pruning Strategies ‣ PrunedLoRA: Robust Gradient-Based structured pruning for Low-rank Adaptation in Fine-tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the metrics of perturbation error for activation-based pruning and gradient-based pruning strategies derive from (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2 Related Work ‣ PrunedLoRA: Robust Gradient-Based structured pruning for Low-rank Adaptation in Fine-tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) and (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2 Related Work ‣ PrunedLoRA: Robust Gradient-Based structured pruning for Low-rank Adaptation in Fine-tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), respectively. Noticeably, even though we can set the same precision of the perturbation error for different pruning strategies (under Def <a href="#Thmdefinition1" title="Definition 1 (𝜀-Perturbation Error). ‣ B.1 Analysis for Gradient-based Pruning versus Activation-based Pruning ‣ Appendix B Analysis for structured pruning Strategies ‣ PrunedLoRA: Robust Gradient-Based structured pruning for Low-rank Adaptation in Fine-tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> ), we cannot know how the perturbation error of different pruning strategies contributes to the overall loss. Intuitively, gradient-based strategies emphasize preserving the global correlation between <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{{\bm{W}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{{\bm{W}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑾</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which suggests greater robustness to weight perturbations for the overall loss. However, this intuition has not yet been formally established. In the following, we conduct an analysis on a single attention module to provide theoretical justification for this claim. It is an official statement of Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 1 (Unofficial Statement). ‣ 3.2 The Robustness of Gradient-based structured pruning ‣ 3 Methods ‣ PrunedLoRA: Robust Gradient-Based structured pruning for Low-rank Adaptation in Fine-tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2</span></span><span id="Thmproposition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproposition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Official Statement) 
In a single attention module, if we assume each module of <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Q,K,V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Q,K,V)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying perturbation error <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> in activation-based strategies, respectively, the overall loss would be linear w.r.t the perturbation error up to the magnitude of each module. However, if they satisfy the perturbation error <math id="Thmproposition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> in gradient-based strategies, the overall loss would be linear the perturbation error and independent of the magnitude for each module.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof:</span>   Given an input <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d_{\text{model}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>model</mtext></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d_{\text{model}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the query, key, and value module of a single attention module are obtained through three separate linear
transformations:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q=XW_{Q},\quad K=XW_{K},\quad V=XW_{V}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q=XW_{Q},\quad K=XW_{K},\quad V=XW_{V},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{Q},W_{K},W_{V}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{model}}\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>model</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{Q},W_{K},W_{V}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{model}}\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> are
trainable weight matrices, and <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is the dimensionality of a single
attention head. Here, we assume these three modules have the same dimension. The attention output is then computed as</p>
<table id="A2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z=\text{softmax}\!\left(\frac{QK^{\top}}{\sqrt{d}}\right)V." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:3.720em;" width="3.720em"><mtext>softmax</mtext></mpadded><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z=\text{softmax}\!\left(\frac{QK^{\top}}{\sqrt{d}}\right)V.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The scaling factor <math id="A2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\sqrt{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\sqrt{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is introduced to prevent <math id="A2.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="QK^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">QK^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> from growing too large in magnitude,
which would otherwise make the softmax distribution extremely peaked and lead to unstable gradients. Given a weight vector <math id="A2.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{1},x_{2},\cdots,x_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{1},x_{2},\cdots,x_{d})</annotation></semantics></math>, the softmax function will transform the <math id="A2.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th element in the vector as</p>
<table id="A2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{softmax}(x_{i})=\frac{\exp(x_{i})}{\sum_{j}\exp(x_{j})}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{softmax}(x_{i})=\frac{\exp(x_{i})}{\sum_{j}\exp(x_{j})},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">which transforms a vector of real numbers into a probability distribution.
In the attention mechanism, the softmax ensures that the attention weights assigned to all keys
are non-negative and sum to one.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">First, we will analyze activation-based pruning strategies. If we suppose <math id="A2.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|Q-\widehat{Q}\|_{F}\leq\varepsilon,\|K-\widehat{K}\|_{F}\leq\varepsilon,\|V-\widehat{V}\|_{F}\leq\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|Q-\widehat{Q}\|_{F}\leq\varepsilon,\|K-\widehat{K}\|_{F}\leq\varepsilon,\|V-\widehat{V}\|_{F}\leq\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, i.e., perturbation error in each module is bounded by <math id="A2.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> (See Def <a href="#Thmdefinition1" title="Definition 1 (𝜀-Perturbation Error). ‣ B.1 Analysis for Gradient-based Pruning versus Activation-based Pruning ‣ Appendix B Analysis for structured pruning Strategies ‣ PrunedLoRA: Robust Gradient-Based structured pruning for Low-rank Adaptation in Fine-tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). Then,</p>
<table id="A2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|Z-\widehat{Z}\right\|_{F}\leq\left\|A(V-\widehat{V})\right\|_{F}+\left\|(A-\widehat{A})\widehat{V}\right\|_{F}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|Z-\widehat{Z}\right\|_{F}\leq\left\|A(V-\widehat{V})\right\|_{F}+\left\|(A-\widehat{A})\widehat{V}\right\|_{F},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p6.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=\mathrm{softmax}\left(\frac{QK^{\top}}{\sqrt{d}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=\mathrm{softmax}\left(\frac{QK^{\top}}{\sqrt{d}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{A}=\mathrm{softmax}\left(\frac{\widehat{Q}\widehat{K}^{\top}}{\sqrt{d}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>softmax</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{A}=\mathrm{softmax}\left(\frac{\widehat{Q}\widehat{K}^{\top}}{\sqrt{d}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. The first term is at most <math id="A2.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> due to the fact that <math id="A2.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|A\|\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|A\|\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. The second term depends on the mismatch between <math id="A2.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> after pruning:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|QK^{\top}-\widehat{Q}\widehat{K}^{\top}\right\|_{F}\leq\left\|Q\right\|\cdot\left\|K-\widehat{K}\right\|_{F}+\|K\|\cdot\left\|Q-\widehat{Q}\right\|_{F}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">‖</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>K</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|QK^{\top}-\widehat{Q}\widehat{K}^{\top}\right\|_{F}\leq\left\|Q\right\|\cdot\left\|K-\widehat{K}\right\|_{F}+\|K\|\cdot\left\|Q-\widehat{Q}\right\|_{F}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p6.3" class="ltx_p">This shows that the error in <math id="A2.SS1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> scales linearly with both <math id="A2.SS1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> and the magnitude of <math id="A2.SS1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, leading to an overall bound:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\|Z-\widehat{Z}\right\|_{F}\leq\left(1+\frac{\|Q\|+\|K\|}{\sqrt{d}}\cdot\|\widehat{V}\|\right)\varepsilon" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\|Z-\widehat{Z}\right\|_{F}\leq\left(1+\frac{\|Q\|+\|K\|}{\sqrt{d}}\cdot\|\widehat{V}\|\right)\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, under the perturbation error of gradient-based tuning strategies, if we assume that <math id="A2.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(Q,K,V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(Q,K,V)</annotation></semantics></math> is the loss of a single attention module, we know that</p>
<table id="A2.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|\mathcal{L}(Q,K,V)-\mathcal{L}(\widehat{Q},\widehat{K},\widehat{V})\right|\leq 3\varepsilon," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|\mathcal{L}(Q,K,V)-\mathcal{L}(\widehat{Q},\widehat{K},\widehat{V})\right|\leq 3\varepsilon,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p7.2" class="ltx_p">which is a direct consequence of the triangle inequality. This concludes the proof.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we will analyze how pruning a single weight matrix <math id="A2.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑾</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> affects the overall loss function <math id="A2.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> in the general cases.
Assume that the loss function <math id="A2.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A2.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz continuous (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> for formal definitions).</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">For gradient-based pruning methods, if the pruning procedure introduces an <math id="A2.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-level perturbation error to the weights, the resulting loss change is at most <math id="A2.SS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., the approximation error in the loss is directly proportional to the perturbation error. This result is consistent with the conclusion we established on the toy model.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, for activation-based pruning methods, pruning a weight matrix with perturbation error <math id="A2.SS1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> yields a change in the loss that is bounded by <math id="A2.SS1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A2.SS1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is the Lipschitz constant of <math id="A2.SS1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. Recent work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> has shown that both the lower and upper bounds of the Lipschitz constant tend to increase as training progresses. Consequently, the sensitivity of the loss to perturbations induced by activation-based pruning can escalate over the course of fine-tuning, making its impact more difficult to control compared to gradient-based approaches.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, in the toy model, we can explicitly observe the impact of pruning multiple matrices under both gradient-based and activation-based strategies. The larger the matrix magnitude, the greater the error inflation in the overall loss function in activation-based methods. More generally, when considering a single weight matrix in any loss function, our analysis also highlights that activation-based methods are influenced by the Lipschitz constant, in contrast to gradient-based methods.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Analysis for the Masking Pruning and Weight Update in the Problem <a href="#S3.E6" title="In 3.2 The Robustness of Gradient-based structured pruning ‣ 3 Methods ‣ PrunedLoRA: Robust Gradient-Based structured pruning for Low-rank Adaptation in Fine-tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a></h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this part, we will provide a detailed analysis of the Problem (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In 3.2 The Robustness of Gradient-based structured pruning ‣ 3 Methods ‣ PrunedLoRA: Robust Gradient-Based structured pruning for Low-rank Adaptation in Fine-tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) as</p>
<table id="A2.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}\mathcal{M}_{s},{\bm{\delta}}={\arg\min}_{\mathcal{M}_{s},{\bm{\delta}}}&amp;\left\langle\nabla_{{\bm{W}}}\mathcal{L},{\bm{\delta}}\right\rangle+\frac{1}{2}tr({\bm{\delta}}\widehat{{\bm{H}}}\,{\bm{\delta}}^{\top})\\
\textit{s.t.}\quad&{\bm{\delta}}_{:,\mathcal{M}_{s}}=-{\bm{W}}_{:,\mathcal{M}_{s}}.\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝜹</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>min</mi><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜹</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝑾</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜹</mi></mrow><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝜹</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝜹</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">s.t.</mtext></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>𝜹</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}\mathcal{M}_{s},{\bm{\delta}}={\arg\min}_{\mathcal{M}_{s},{\bm{\delta}}}&amp;\left\langle\nabla_{{\bm{W}}}\mathcal{L},{\bm{\delta}}\right\rangle+\frac{1}{2}tr({\bm{\delta}}\widehat{{\bm{H}}}\,{\bm{\delta}}^{\top})\\
\textit{s.t.}\quad&amp;{\bm{\delta}}_{:,\mathcal{M}_{s}}=-{\bm{W}}_{:,\mathcal{M}_{s}}.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with optimal solutions for pruning selection <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> and weight update <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\delta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜹</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\delta}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Here, for simplicity, we denote <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{{\bm{W}}}\mathcal{L}({\bm{W}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝑾</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{{\bm{W}}}\mathcal{L}({\bm{W}})</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{{\bm{W}}}\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝑾</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{{\bm{W}}}\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. The corresponding Lagrange problem is</p>
<table id="A2.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E13.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\left\langle\nabla_{{\bm{W}}}\mathcal{L},{\bm{\delta}}\right\rangle+\frac{1}{2}tr({\bm{\delta}}\widehat{{\bm{H}}}{\bm{\delta}}^{\top})+\langle{\bm{\Lambda}},({\bm{\delta}})_{:,\mathcal{M}_{s}}+{\bm{W}}_{:,\mathcal{M}_{s}}\rangle," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝑾</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜹</mi><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>t</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜹</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msup><mi>𝜹</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>𝚲</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜹</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝑾</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left\langle\nabla_{{\bm{W}}}\mathcal{L},{\bm{\delta}}\right\rangle+\frac{1}{2}tr({\bm{\delta}}\widehat{{\bm{H}}}{\bm{\delta}}^{\top})+\langle{\bm{\Lambda}},({\bm{\delta}})_{:,\mathcal{M}_{s}}+{\bm{W}}_{:,\mathcal{M}_{s}}\rangle,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\Lambda}}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚲</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\Lambda}}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a Lagrange multiplier. Under first order condition of <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\delta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜹</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\delta}}</annotation></semantics></math>, it implies</p>
<table id="A2.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}\nabla_{{\bm{W}}}\mathcal{L}+{\bm{\delta}}\widehat{{\bm{H}}}+{\bm{\Lambda}}P_{\mathcal{M}_{s}}=0,\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝑾</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝜹</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝚲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}\nabla_{{\bm{W}}}\mathcal{L}+{\bm{\delta}}\widehat{{\bm{H}}}+{\bm{\Lambda}}P_{\mathcal{M}_{s}}=0,\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\mathcal{M}_{s}}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\mathcal{M}_{s}}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a diagonal matrix whose <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th diagonal entry is <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> if the <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th column is pruned and <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise. Then we have</p>
<table id="A2.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}{\bm{\delta}}=-\left(\nabla_{{\bm{W}}}\mathcal{L}+{\bm{\Lambda}}P_{\mathcal{M}_{s}}\right)\widehat{{\bm{H}}}^{-1}=-\nabla_{{\bm{W}}}\mathcal{L}\&gt;\widehat{{\bm{H}}}^{-1}-{\bm{\Lambda}}P_{\mathcal{M}_{s}}\&gt;\widehat{{\bm{H}}}^{-1}.\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi>𝜹</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝑾</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝚲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝑾</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.220em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝚲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}{\bm{\delta}}=-\left(\nabla_{{\bm{W}}}\mathcal{L}+{\bm{\Lambda}}P_{\mathcal{M}_{s}}\right)\widehat{{\bm{H}}}^{-1}=-\nabla_{{\bm{W}}}\mathcal{L}\&gt;\widehat{{\bm{H}}}^{-1}-{\bm{\Lambda}}P_{\mathcal{M}_{s}}\&gt;\widehat{{\bm{H}}}^{-1}.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Then we could put the expression of <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\delta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜹</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\delta}}</annotation></semantics></math> back into the structure constraint (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3.2 The Robustness of Gradient-based structured pruning ‣ 3 Methods ‣ PrunedLoRA: Robust Gradient-Based structured pruning for Low-rank Adaptation in Fine-tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) and get</p>
<table id="A2.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}{\bm{\Lambda}}=\left({\bm{W}}_{:,\mathcal{M}_{s}}-(\nabla_{{\bm{W}}}\mathcal{L}\&gt;\widehat{{\bm{H}}}^{-1})_{:,\mathcal{M}_{s}}\right)\left((\widehat{{\bm{H}}}^{-1})_{\mathcal{M}_{s},\mathcal{M}_{s}}\right)^{-1}.\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>𝚲</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝑾</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo><mo>(</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}{\bm{\Lambda}}=\left({\bm{W}}_{:,\mathcal{M}_{s}}-(\nabla_{{\bm{W}}}\mathcal{L}\&gt;\widehat{{\bm{H}}}^{-1})_{:,\mathcal{M}_{s}}\right)\left((\widehat{{\bm{H}}}^{-1})_{\mathcal{M}_{s},\mathcal{M}_{s}}\right)^{-1}.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Finally, putting the form of <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\Lambda}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\Lambda}}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#A2.E16" title="In B.2 Analysis for the Masking Pruning and Weight Update in the Problem ‣ Appendix B Analysis for structured pruning Strategies ‣ PrunedLoRA: Robust Gradient-Based structured pruning for Low-rank Adaptation in Fine-tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>) back into (<a href="#A2.E14" title="In B.2 Analysis for the Masking Pruning and Weight Update in the Problem ‣ Appendix B Analysis for structured pruning Strategies ‣ PrunedLoRA: Robust Gradient-Based structured pruning for Low-rank Adaptation in Fine-tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>), we could get <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\delta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜹</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\delta}}</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="A2.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}{\bm{\delta}}&amp;=-\nabla_{{\bm{W}}}\mathcal{L}\&gt;\widehat{{\bm{H}}}^{-1}-{\bm{W}}_{:,\mathcal{M}_{s}}\left((\widehat{{\bm{H}}}^{-1})_{\mathcal{M}_{s},\mathcal{M}_{s}}\right)^{-1}(\widehat{{\bm{H}}}^{-1})_{\mathcal{M}_{s},:}\\
&amp;\quad+(\nabla_{{\bm{W}}}\mathcal{L}\widehat{{\bm{H}}}^{-1})_{:,\mathcal{M}_{s}}\left((\widehat{{\bm{H}}}^{-1})_{\mathcal{M}_{s},\mathcal{M}_{s}}\right)^{-1}(\widehat{{\bm{H}}}^{-1})_{\mathcal{M}_{s},:}.\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mi>𝜹</mi></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝑾</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝑾</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}{\bm{\delta}}&amp;=-\nabla_{{\bm{W}}}\mathcal{L}\&gt;\widehat{{\bm{H}}}^{-1}-{\bm{W}}_{:,\mathcal{M}_{s}}\left((\widehat{{\bm{H}}}^{-1})_{\mathcal{M}_{s},\mathcal{M}_{s}}\right)^{-1}(\widehat{{\bm{H}}}^{-1})_{\mathcal{M}_{s},:}\\
&amp;\quad+(\nabla_{{\bm{W}}}\mathcal{L}\widehat{{\bm{H}}}^{-1})_{:,\mathcal{M}_{s}}\left((\widehat{{\bm{H}}}^{-1})_{\mathcal{M}_{s},\mathcal{M}_{s}}\right)^{-1}(\widehat{{\bm{H}}}^{-1})_{\mathcal{M}_{s},:}.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">structured pruning methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>, <a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">80</a>]</cite> remove entire structured components of a network, facilitating efficient GPU speedups <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>. Utilizing the gradient of the overall loss function in training, termed gradient-based methods, can be robust for eliminating the change of loss under the impact of weight perturbation in pruning. Gradients of weight are computed during the normal optimization process; one can easily reuse those for determining weight importance efficiently. Within the context of gradient-based pruning, we want to further explain the development of existing methods and clarify the difference with our effort in this paper. Most of the works in the literature use an important score to select the pruning structure <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>, <a href="#bib.bib89" title="" class="ltx_ref">89</a>, <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite>. They provide refined pruning selection but do not further eliminate the influence of structured pruning. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">74</a>]</cite> combines distillation with pruning to improve performance and erase the impact of structured pruning, but they require minimizing the KL-divergence of two distributions and cannot find a closed-form solution.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Inspired by Optimal Brain Surgeon, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> propose a weight update after model pruning in the context of model compression to further eliminate the influence of pruning. Since their analysis is established for one-dimensional weight vectors, the pruning metric is hard to interpret. In contrast, we establish the analysis for the weight matrix and provide a grounded interpretation for the pruning selection and weight update (See Sec <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The Robustness of Gradient-based structured pruning ‣ 3 Methods ‣ PrunedLoRA: Robust Gradient-Based structured pruning for Low-rank Adaptation in Fine-tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg2" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span> Gradient-based structured pruning with Weight Update. We prune the layer matrix <math id="alg2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑾</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> with column-wise sparsity <math id="alg2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> given the gradient <math id="alg2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\bm{W}}\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝑾</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\bm{W}}\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> and the Hessian matrix <math id="alg2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{{\bm{H}}}=(\nabla_{{\bm{W}}}\mathcal{L})^{T}\nabla_{{\bm{W}}}\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝑾</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝑾</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{{\bm{H}}}=(\nabla_{{\bm{W}}}\mathcal{L})^{T}\nabla_{{\bm{W}}}\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
<div id="alg2.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg2.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg2.l1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 1: Search pruning columns with sparsity <math id="alg2.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>

<table id="A2.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\arg\min_{\mathcal{M}_{s}}\;\mathrm{tr}\!\left(({\bm{W}}-\nabla_{\bm{W}}\mathcal{L}\&gt;\widehat{{\bm{H}}}^{-1})_{:,\mathcal{M}_{s}}\left((\widehat{{\bm{H}}}^{-1})_{\mathcal{M}_{s},\mathcal{M}_{s}}\right)^{-1}({\bm{W}}-\nabla_{\bm{W}}\mathcal{L}\&gt;\widehat{{\bm{H}}}^{-1})_{:,\mathcal{M}_{s}}^{\top}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>arg</mi><munder><mi>min</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></munder><mpadded style="width:0.670em;" width="0.670em"><mi>tr</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝑾</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝑾</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\arg\min_{\mathcal{M}_{s}}\;\mathrm{tr}\!\left(({\bm{W}}-\nabla_{\bm{W}}\mathcal{L}\&gt;\widehat{{\bm{H}}}^{-1})_{:,\mathcal{M}_{s}}\left((\widehat{{\bm{H}}}^{-1})_{\mathcal{M}_{s},\mathcal{M}_{s}}\right)^{-1}({\bm{W}}-\nabla_{\bm{W}}\mathcal{L}\&gt;\widehat{{\bm{H}}}^{-1})_{:,\mathcal{M}_{s}}^{\top}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg2.l2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 2: Compute optimal update.</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
            Given <math id="alg2.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, compute update <math id="alg2.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\delta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜹</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\delta}}</annotation></semantics></math>:

<table id="A2.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\bm{\delta}}=-\nabla_{\bm{W}}\mathcal{L}\widehat{{\bm{H}}}^{-1}-({\bm{W}}-\nabla_{\bm{W}}\mathcal{L}\widehat{{\bm{H}}}^{-1})_{:,\mathcal{M}_{s}}\big((\widehat{{\bm{H}}}^{-1})_{\mathcal{M}_{s},\mathcal{M}_{s}}\big)^{-1}(\widehat{{\bm{H}}}^{-1})_{\mathcal{M}_{s},:}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜹</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝑾</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝑾</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>:</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\delta}}=-\nabla_{\bm{W}}\mathcal{L}\widehat{{\bm{H}}}^{-1}-({\bm{W}}-\nabla_{\bm{W}}\mathcal{L}\widehat{{\bm{H}}}^{-1})_{:,\mathcal{M}_{s}}\big((\widehat{{\bm{H}}}^{-1})_{\mathcal{M}_{s},\mathcal{M}_{s}}\big)^{-1}(\widehat{{\bm{H}}}^{-1})_{\mathcal{M}_{s},:}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg2.l4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 3: Update model.</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
            Set <math id="alg2.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}\leftarrow{\bm{W}}+{\bm{\delta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>𝜹</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}\leftarrow{\bm{W}}+{\bm{\delta}}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg2.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg2.l6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 4: Iterate or finalize.</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
              
            If multi-round pruning, repeat Steps 1–3 until target sparsity/rank is reached. Otherwise, output <math id="alg2.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑾</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Experiment</h2>

<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Hyperparameter</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For both the <em id="A3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">natural language generation task</em> and <em id="A3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">natural language understanding task</em>, we use the following choice of hyperparameters in supervised fine-tuning. All experiments are conducted on NVIDIA H100 GPUs.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In supervised fine-tuning, we use the standard optimizer AdamW <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite> with default hyperparameters <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{1}=0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{1}=0.9</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{2}=0.999" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.999</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{2}=0.999</annotation></semantics></math>, and weight decay set to zero. A cosine learning rate schedule with a warm-up ratio of 0.03 is adopted. LoRA adapters are inserted into the <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{Q,K,V,O\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{Q,K,V,O\}</annotation></semantics></math> projection layers. We fine-tune each task for <span id="A3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">three epochs</span>, with a maximum of <span id="A3.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">5000</span> training steps per epoch. During the fine-tuning process, we will conduct structured pruning to obtain low-rank adapters.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For the choice of learning rate, we perform grid search over <math id="A3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1e-5,5e-6,1e-6\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1e-5,5e-6,1e-6\}</annotation></semantics></math> and report the best result among these learning rates.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Other Hyperparameters: Sequence Length <math id="A3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> = 128, train batch size 4, precision FP16.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Pruning Strategy</h3>

<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dynamic Pruning.</span> Motivated by Fig. <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3.1 Motivation ‣ 3 Methods ‣ PrunedLoRA: Robust Gradient-Based structured pruning for Low-rank Adaptation in Fine-tuning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we observe that higher-rank LoRA adapters (<math id="A3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑨</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{B}}</annotation></semantics></math>) achieve better empirical performance with smaller variance.
Based on this observation, we propose to prune adapters starting from higher-rank spaces.
Specifically, we initialize adapters with rank <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in\{128,256,512\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>128,256,512</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in\{128,256,512\}</annotation></semantics></math> and progressively prune them down to rank <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>64</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64</annotation></semantics></math>, corresponding to <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50\%</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="75\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>75</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">75\%</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="87.5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>87.5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">87.5\%</annotation></semantics></math> sparsity, respectively.
We also explore more aggressive settings (e.g., pruning from <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math>).
Pruning is performed in a structured manner, controlled by two hyperparameters: the pruning interval <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and the number of columns removed per step <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For example, with <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}=10</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{2}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{2}=2</annotation></semantics></math>, we prune two columns every ten training steps.
Once the remaining columns reach the target rank budget (default: <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>64</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64</annotation></semantics></math>), pruning is terminated.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Adaptive Choice of Hyperparameter.</span> Importantly, as rank dynamically changes during training, the scaling factor <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> must remain stable.
While vanilla LoRA typically sets <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=16</annotation></semantics></math>, we find this choice suboptimal for higher-rank initializations.
To address it, we perform a grid search over a large range and identify that <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\{r/2,r,2r\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\{r/2,r,2r\}</annotation></semantics></math> can achieve the better performance, where <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> is the current rank in LoRA. The hyperparameter <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> will be proportional to <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> over the training process.</p>
</div>
</section>
